  "worked_solution": "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-3x+2}=\\frac{1-1}{(1)^{2}-3(1)+2}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-3x+2} & =\\frac{x-1}{(x-1)(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x-1}}{\\cancel{(x-1)}(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-3x+2} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{1}{x-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-2} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
